---
import DocsFrame from '../../components/DocsFrame.astro';

const faqs = [
	{
		question: 'What is PollyPM in one sentence?',
		answer:
			'PollyPM is a control layer for running several AI agent sessions as one managed software delivery system instead of a collection of unrelated chats and terminals.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Who is PollyPM for?',
		answer:
			'It is for engineering teams, tooling groups, and operators who already use AI agents and need better coordination, health visibility, and Git hygiene when work starts running in parallel.',
	},
	{
		question: 'How is PollyPM different from just opening more chat tabs?',
		answer:
			'More chat tabs increase throughput only until coordination breaks down. PollyPM adds session management, worktree isolation, provider routing, and operator visibility so parallel work remains usable.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Do I need tmux to use PollyPM?',
		answer:
			'For the operating model described in these docs, yes. PollyPM expects tmux-backed sessions so work can continue, reconnect cleanly, and remain observable after the original terminal goes away.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Why does PollyPM use Git worktrees?',
		answer:
			'Worktrees give each lane its own checkout so several agents can edit code safely at the same time without stomping on the same branch or working directory.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Can PollyPM use more than one provider?',
		answer:
			'Yes. PollyPM is designed to keep provider routing explicit so a project can continue when one model, account, or vendor becomes unavailable or rate-limited.',
	},
	{
		question: 'What should a first PollyPM rollout look like?',
		answer:
			'Start with one small project and two lanes. Verify launch, monitor output, worktree isolation, and operator handoff before you expand to more repos or more concurrent sessions.',
	},
	{
		question: 'What usually goes wrong first?',
		answer:
			'The most common early failures are missing provider credentials, incorrect repository paths, vague session names, and untested fallback assumptions.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Should PollyPM replace code review or human judgment?',
		answer:
			'No. PollyPM is there to manage execution and coordination. Operators still decide task boundaries, review code, and make shipping decisions.',
	},
];

const quickRoutes = [
	{
		label: 'Ready to install',
		title: 'Start with onboarding',
		description: 'Go straight to the first-run guide if you already understand the value proposition and need the operator workflow.',
		href: '/docs/getting-started',
	},
	{
		label: 'Still evaluating',
		title: 'Read concepts next',
		description: 'Use the architecture page if you need to understand the model behind sessions, worktrees, and operator checkpoints.',
		href: '/docs/concepts',
	},
	{
		label: 'Need commands',
		title: 'Open CLI reference',
		description: 'Jump to the command and config surface when the pilot is already active and you need practical examples.',
		href: '/docs/cli-reference',
	},
];
---

<DocsFrame
	title="FAQ | PollyPM Docs"
	description="Frequently asked questions about PollyPM, its operating model, and how to roll it out."
	heroTitle="Common PollyPM questions."
	heroSummary="This page answers the practical questions teams usually ask when they are deciding whether PollyPM fits their workflow or when they are setting up the first pilot."
	currentPath="/docs/faq"
	pills={['FAQ', 'Rollout', 'Operations']}
>
	<section class="docs-section">
		<div class="docs-section-head">
			<span class="docs-eyebrow">FAQ</span>
			<h2>Start here if you want the short version before diving deeper.</h2>
			<p class="docs-section-copy">These are the practical questions teams ask when they are evaluating the model or planning a first rollout.</p>
		</div>

		<div class="faq-spotlight">
			<div class="faq-spotlight-copy">
				<span class="docs-card-label">Fast orientation</span>
				<strong>The short answer: PollyPM makes multi-agent work operable.</strong>
				<p>Most teams do not need more raw agent output. They need better coordination, clearer health signals, safer Git boundaries, and a way to recover when a lane drifts.</p>
			</div>

			<div class="faq-route-grid">
				{
					quickRoutes.map((route) => (
						<a class="docs-card docs-card-link faq-route-card" href={route.href}>
							<span class="docs-card-label">{route.label}</span>
							<strong>{route.title}</strong>
							<p>{route.description}</p>
						</a>
					))
				}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="docs-section">
		<div class="docs-section-head">
			<span class="docs-eyebrow">Questions</span>
			<h2>The practical questions teams usually ask first.</h2>
		</div>

		<div class="docs-grid two-up faq-grid">
			{
				faqs.map((item, index) => (
					<article class="docs-card faq-card">
						<span class="docs-card-label">Question {index + 1}</span>
						<strong>{item.question}</strong>
						<p>{item.answer}</p>
					</article>
				))
			}
		</div>

		<div class="docs-cta">
			<div>
				<span class="docs-card-label">Next page</span>
				<strong>Read the FAQ, then choose the right next doc.</strong>
				<p class="docs-section-copy">If you are still evaluating the model, read concepts next. If you are ready to run the tool, go straight to getting started or the CLI reference.</p>
			</div>

			<div class="docs-actions">
				<a class="docs-button primary" href="/docs/getting-started">Open getting started</a>
				<a class="docs-button secondary" href="/docs/concepts">Read concepts</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</DocsFrame>

<style>
	.faq-spotlight {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.faq-spotlight-copy,
	.faq-route-grid,
	.faq-card {
		position: relative;
	}

	.faq-spotlight-copy {
		padding: 1.3rem;
		border-radius: 30px;
		border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
		background:
			radial-gradient(circle at top right, rgba(255, 131, 87, 0.14), transparent 28%),
			linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02)),
			rgba(9, 17, 31, 0.82);
		box-shadow:
			0 28px 65px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.22),
			inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
	}

	.faq-spotlight-copy strong {
		display: block;
		margin-top: 0.7rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: -0.05em;
	}

	.faq-spotlight-copy p {
		margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
		color: rgba(236, 243, 255, 0.76);
		line-height: 1.72;
	}

	.faq-route-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.faq-route-card {
		min-height: 100%;
	}

	.faq-grid .faq-card:nth-child(3n + 2) {
		transform: translateY(1.6rem);
	}

	.faq-grid .faq-card:nth-child(3n) {
		transform: translateY(0.8rem);
	}

	@media (max-width: 1100px) {
		.faq-spotlight,
		.faq-route-grid {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}

		.faq-grid .faq-card:nth-child(3n + 2),
		.faq-grid .faq-card:nth-child(3n) {
			transform: none;
		}
	}
</style>
